WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.620
Now throwing heads is a real thing.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.380
It's definitely an intimidation tactic.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:11.380
When the First Crusade
reached Nicaea in 1097,

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.390
both the attackers and defenders
threw heads at each other.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:16.254
Hi, I'm Dr. Michael Fulton.

00:00:16.254 --> 00:00:21.000
I am an expert at siege warfare
and medieval fortifications.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.070
Today, we're going to be
looking at castle battle scenes

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.083
in movies and TV and
judging how real they are.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.560
There's nothing far-fetched

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.940
about sneaking into a castle or town.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.100
There are ways to do that,

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.630
whether you're disguised as somebody

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.130
or you're hiding in something.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.017
Kind of runs to the edge and says,

00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:48.610
"Hey you, stop that."

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:51.260
There's a traditional
phenomenon called castle watch.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.240
You're really just watching,

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.920
making sure that nobody
sneaks up on your castle.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.598
What you're trying to
defend your castle against

00:00:57.598 --> 00:00:59.010
is a large army,

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.270
because it's very hard to take a castle

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:03.690
unless you have a large army.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.740
It's hard to sneak up on a castle.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.230
If you're coming with an army,

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.897
people know that army is coming.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.580
I think this is supposed
to be Kildrummy Castle,

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.780
which, by this point, had a
very elaborate gate system.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.860
So just opening that gate

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.760
would have been quite a challenge.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.300
Everybody's going to go to that gate.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.640
A gate like that would have
been a whole structure.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.030
So each gate to that structure

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.947
would presumably have been
locked by someone on the inside.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.820
The idea of this small
party trying to rush

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:46.930
against this small group
that are in the Great Hall,

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.210
I kind of don't understand,
because a castle of this scale

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.600
is going to have a lot more defenders,

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.830
and you're going to have
to overcome them all.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.369
So your priority is really seizing

00:01:56.369 --> 00:01:59.210
the strategic parts of the castle.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.390
The Great Hall is not really one of them.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.280
Sleeping in the, or on the
floor of the Great Hall

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:05.790
was a traditional thing to do.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.260
It's starting to go out of
fashion by this point in history.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:13.300
But if you were to look back
at say the 11th, 12th century,

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.130
the king's retainers,
or the lord's retainers,

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.610
his kind of high-ranking guys

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.450
would have a lot of drinking parties

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.700
and then just kind of pass
out on the floor around.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.853
There would be weapons around.

00:02:28.853 --> 00:02:32.344
You wouldn't need to
necessarily don your armor

00:02:32.344 --> 00:02:35.160
in order to defend a castle like this.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.730
That's much more helpful
in an open battle.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:40.073
Maybe a 2 if I'm being generous.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.200
Hey, archer, stay alive.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:45.120
The little screens there,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.703
those were very important,
because they stopped arrows.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.380
The moat or the ditch,

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:58.030
this is the most basic
fortification element,

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.555
other than some kind of a basic wall.

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:02.617
This was supposed to be
the castle at Chalus.

00:03:02.617 --> 00:03:04.350
It's up on a hill,

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.960
so I'm not sure if it
would have needed a ditch.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.600
But if it looked at all like this,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.330
where the ground is kind
of nice and approachable

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:12.690
leading up, you're absolutely
going to have a ditch,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.740
because it's so easy.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.030
They put a lot of effort in
hanging these incendiaries

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.760
on the gate.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.093
Why don't you just put it
at the bottom of the gate?

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:30.560
Setting gates on fire is
a legitimate tactic here.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.260
It largely predates this period, though.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.690
If you have incendiaries
during this period,

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:39.558
they are more likely
to look like tar than,

00:03:39.558 --> 00:03:41.500
well, presumably like gasoline,

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.000
because you have to refine it.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:46.769
Men: Heave!

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:47.602
(ram banging)

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:49.770
The portcullis has
disappeared for some reason.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.820
So the portcullis designed like that,

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:53.670
with that lattice structure,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.160
that was coming into
fashion around this time.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:02.160
Whether it is wood that has
been kind of coated with metal,

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:05.280
or plated, or whether it's entirely metal,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.590
you see both variations.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:11.410
The whole point of it,
though, is that it is open

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:13.710
so you can stick stuff
through it if you want.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:14.543
And if it's metal,

00:04:14.543 --> 00:04:16.460
presumably it means you
can't set it on fire,

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:19.060
which is critical here, right?

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.213
Traditionally, the gate
is the weakest part

00:04:21.213 --> 00:04:24.450
of a fortified enclosure,
because it's a hole, right?

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:28.090
So you need to strengthen
that hole to make sure

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:30.160
that it's not a point of
weakness or vulnerability.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.050
So through the 13th century,

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.760
gates actually become the
strongest part of a castle

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.270
instead of the weakest part,

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.020
just because of the level of
development that goes into it.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.270
Kind of walking up to a gate,
trying to set it on fire,

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.540
trying to use a ram against it.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.230
They've had a long time to think about

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.480
these kind of siege tactics

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.230
and to develop fortifications accordingly.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.550
So it's kind of anachronistic order.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.420
The siege tactics are
already a little bit outdated

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:56.880
in this representation.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.170
These guys are trying.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.630
They haven't pulled it all
together as best as they could.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:04.130
I'd give them somewhere, 5, 6.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:10.960
These are terrible catapults.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:13.760
The stones seem to somehow speed up

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.440
as they leave the basket.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.910
It should slow down from that point.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:20.090
If you look at how big
this chunk of stone is,

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.020
it's significantly larger than the engine

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.853
that's supposed to be throwing it.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.540
Now, throwing heads is a real thing.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.810
This is an element of
psychological warfare.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:33.540
It's definitely an intimidation tactic.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:38.540
When the First Crusade
reached Nicaea in 1097,

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.610
both the attackers and defenders
threw heads at each other.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:45.650
It took place more often when
you had groups facing off

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:47.650
from different cultures.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.220
You don't put a trebuchet,

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.070
a big, counterweight-style trebuchet,

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.730
on top of a tower for a few reasons.

00:05:58.730 --> 00:05:59.890
It is heavy.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:02.070
So the tower actually has to
be able to support the weight

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:02.950
of the engine.

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.930
You get a little bit more range,

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:08.020
but that horizontal range is actually less

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:12.000
than the vertical height that
you've elevated the engine.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.480
If you put it on the
ground behind the tower,

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.190
all of a sudden you
don't have to worry about

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.400
supporting this engine
and all it stresses.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.620
And you actually get the
shielding of the wall in front

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.280
to protect it from the enemy's machines,

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.630
who are presumably trying to break it up.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:30.380
There is this desire or
need to make them really big

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:31.530
and really impressive.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.270
So these were important weapons,

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:34.570
but nowhere near as exciting

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:38.070
as Hollywood loves to make them out to be.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.280
To attack a big, curved wall

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.020
is just like attacking any other wall.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:47.040
Rounded towers are designed purposely

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.060
to deflect incoming artillery and such.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.870
Cohesively, a rounded tower is stronger

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.140
because it doesn't have corners.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:57.780
So you don't have the same
kind of points of stress.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:06:59.710
So if you're trying to undermine a tower,

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:04.100
a round tower is slightly
stronger than a square one.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.873
Cities built on cliffs that you'd attack.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:09.700
Why don't you just build
on top of the cliff?

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:14.270
As real as an orc siege of
a make-believe city can be,

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:18.353
I'm still going to have
to give this 1 out of 10.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.030
Night attacks can take place.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:26.410
There's nothing to say that they didn't

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:27.510
or that they shouldn't.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:30.330
Are they trying to go through a gate?

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:34.040
There are three ways to overcome a wall.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:37.030
You can go over it, in
which case you use ladders,

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:38.490
siege towers, stuff like that.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:42.220
You can go under it, which
is not a great strategy

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.180
because then you're kind of
emerging on the other side

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:45.456
one by one if you tunnel under.

00:07:45.456 --> 00:07:49.140
Or you can go through, and
the best way to go through

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:50.650
is actually to undermine the wall.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.410
Dig a big cavity underneath the wall.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.800
You prop up the wall
above with wooden posts.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:57.470
You throw a bunch of combustibles in.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:59.580
All that support gets burnt out.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.747
The wall above falls down.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:08.010
Lighting arrows on fire like
this, Hollywood loves to do it.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.150
It serves no practical value

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:15.150
unless you're trying to set a
thatched roof cottage on fire.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.730
So they kind of got up to
the base of the wall here.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:24.730
This is a safe spot, in so
much as it's hard for archers

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:26.600
to shoot arrows at them from here.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.600
But you can just drop
some stones over on them.

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:29.750
It's economical.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:33.450
If you consider that every
arrow has to be made by hand,

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:35.570
that's not a short
process to make an arrow.

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:37.419
So when they're throwing off
all these volleys of arrows

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:39.720
and shooting them off as fast as they can,

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.160
that's not very efficient.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:42.603
You drop a bowling ball on somebody,

00:08:42.603 --> 00:08:45.854
and it stops them in their tracks.

00:08:45.854 --> 00:08:50.060
The Hound: Any man dies with a
clean sword, I'll --- corpse!

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:53.470
Defenders exit a gate and try
and attack the besiegers --

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:55.210
that's reasonably common.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:58.040
The issue here is why.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:00.030
You would only do this if you needed to.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.900
Those attackers didn't have the
numbers they probably needed

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:06.430
to overcome these defenses to begin with.

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:10.970
In the medieval period,
leadership is directly tied

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.460
to military performance and prowess.

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:14.802
So for a commander,

00:09:14.802 --> 00:09:18.090
they have to be aware of what's going on

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.180
if they are kind of a warrior figure,

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:23.460
which the most effective
military kings usually were.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:28.460
So having someone on top of
the wall organizing things,

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.350
definitely not beyond
the realm of possibility.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:34.500
I don't quite see what
they're going to accomplish

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.090
once they reach the base of this wall.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:41.257
So I'm going to give this
one maybe a 3 out of 10.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:48.043
This is the siege of Rochester, 1215.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:54.120
Do whatever you can to
help your side get through,

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.910
because if you're then
left alone on that wall,

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:59.462
you're going to be killed yourself.

00:09:59.462 --> 00:10:01.170
So the very worst thing you can do

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:04.120
is get to the top of the
wall and then be left alone.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.043
Priest: This way.

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.360
Rarely you find women
actually taking a direct role

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:09.410
in the fighting.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:13.450
But you do find relatively
frequent examples

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:16.550
of women will bring weapons and supplies

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.210
to people fighting.

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.290
They bring particularly water and food.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:22.040
So there's absolutely a role

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.000
for these kinds of people to play.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.870
The church is a very
logical place for them to be

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:29.040
if they're not taking
part in the fighting.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.080
At this time, the importance
or the centrality of God

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.680
in a conflict like this
would have been huge.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:37.995
Narrator: They were too cold to fight

00:10:37.995 --> 00:10:39.093
and too hungry.

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:43.150
Sieges would last anywhere
from kind of weeks to months.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:47.420
When an army moves into enemy
territory, if there are crops,

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:48.380
they will destroy them.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:50.730
For those inside the defensive perimeter,

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:53.600
they're forced to make do
with the food that they have.

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:57.020
Very rarely did strongholds
actually fall by force.

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.480
The outer line of defenses might fall,

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.500
but then those that are
still in the innermost part

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:02.333
will surrender.

00:11:02.333 --> 00:11:06.180
If the attackers are not able
to impose a complete blockade,

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.810
there might be actually supplies

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:10.650
coming in to support the
defenders throughout the siege.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:12.620
You get this particularly
when you're talking about

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:14.610
bigger cities being attacked.

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:18.027
I've got to give them a 4 or 5 out of 10.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:23.320
This is not what Paris
looked like at the time.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:26.579
The big church in the
background, this is High Gothic.

00:11:26.579 --> 00:11:28.380
The palace right next to it,

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:31.740
this looks suspiciously like
the papal palace in Avignon,

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:34.990
which is 14th century, not 9th century.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.120
We love using the crossbows.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.650
There's anecdotal evidence here

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:42.550
that they were kind of
introduced to the Franks

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:43.890
at this point.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.740
So you've got guys kind of leaning through

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:47.190
the crenels like this,

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:48.670
which are the spaces between the merlons,

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:49.770
the teeth on the walls.

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:53.060
And at what point they came into use,

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:56.643
these would have been
around at this point.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:01.555
So penthouses like these that
they're using for shelter

00:12:01.555 --> 00:12:03.920
are very important.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:06.347
This is a safe way to kind of
get to the base of the walls

00:12:06.347 --> 00:12:07.460
of the city.

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:10.290
Usually, what you'd use
these for, particularly

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:13.430
in later periods, is
undermining the city walls.

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:15.610
So it's a means of getting
up to the city walls

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:19.027
and then trying to dig a hole under them.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.270
How strong is this gate?

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:25.400
Because really these are just some planks.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.350
Horses are important.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:29.300
The significance of mounted warriors

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:31.000
is really growing throughout this period.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:35.250
In a siege though, horses tend
not to be terribly useful.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:37.090
Eating, they need to be fed.

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:41.173
They produce waste, which
needs to be dealt with.

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:50.090
So siege towers are an important
siege weapon in warfare.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:53.010
They are a great way to get people

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:54.580
into an elevated position.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:56.220
The primary function of a siege tower

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:58.290
really is to elevate attackers,

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:00.930
as opposed to trying to get
them to the top of a wall.

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:02.940
That might be a handy secondary purpose,

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:04.760
hiding behind their parapet.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:08.730
So here, it's very helpful,

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:11.400
but a ladder would be a much cheaper

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:15.200
and cost-effective manner
to get up to the top.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:17.340
So I would rate this somewhere
around, I don't know,

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:18.440
a 3 or a 4.

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:22.773
The siege technologies
aren't exactly that accurate.

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:31.240
It wouldn't be uncommon
to have castle gates open.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:34.120
If you were just guarding this gate

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:36.220
against any kind of troublemakers,

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:38.030
it's very believable that the guards

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:39.700
would be down on the bottom floor.

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:41.410
It's a lot easier to chase somebody down

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:43.827
if you're already down there.

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:50.480
For him to go around killing
people indiscriminately,

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.250
that actually makes sense.

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:57.220
Once someone gets into
the fortified enclosure,

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:59.790
there's no protection for those within,

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:02.730
unless some kind of peace
agreement is prearranged.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:04.150
So the whole point of a castle

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:08.660
is you're having walls
do the work of defenders.

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:11.200
The whole market kind of environment

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.120
would usually be outside the castle.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.310
The environment inside the castle

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:18.550
is catering only to those
really who live there

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:20.530
or might need to take refuge there.

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:23.100
It was a matter of
protecting who was in charge.

00:14:24.342 --> 00:14:28.098
Guard: Now, you're not allowed to enter.

00:14:28.098 --> 00:14:30.330
Lancelot: O, fair one,
behold your humble servant.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:35.330
You don't often find cases
where someone is lauded

00:14:35.610 --> 00:14:39.450
for single-handedly capturing a castle

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:41.490
or some other fortification.

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:44.680
More often, you will
find individuals praised

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:46.378
for their conduct.

00:14:46.378 --> 00:14:50.160
The first person to set
foot on a defender's wall.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.110
So if you're the first
one up on your ladder,

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:53.700
the first one up on your siege tower.

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:55.720
Usually, that person gets special mention.

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.202
To take a castle kind of
like we associate them,

00:14:58.202 --> 00:15:00.400
or we picture them,

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:02.545
you're going to need a force
in at least the hundreds,

00:15:02.545 --> 00:15:04.700
probably the thousands.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:07.470
I have no idea what to offer this one.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:08.970
I believe this is a real castle.

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:13.970
It's setting about a very
kind of rose-colored glasses

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:16.551
day in the life of a castle.

00:15:16.551 --> 00:15:18.718
It's trying. I don't know.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:28.690
Just lobbing arrows at a distance

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:30.960
is not an effective siege tactic

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:32.450
or counter-siege tactic.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:35.520
So when you see archers, they
pull an arrow, they nock it,

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.410
they draw, and then they kind of hold,

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:38.850
then they shoot their arrows all at once.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:42.220
Again, this is inspired
by later musket tactics,

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.910
where you have lines of
musketeers shooting off volleys,

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:48.700
for the same reason that you would wait

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:53.450
until the attackers got closer
to shoot a musket at them.

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:58.240
Well, a ladder is just
propped up against the wall.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.166
They'd actually be quite sturdy

00:16:00.166 --> 00:16:02.810
and you'd reuse them sometimes.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:05.310
So this would have been
a difficult tactic,

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:07.010
but a believable one.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:10.430
So the easiest way to try and make sure

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:11.870
that ladder doesn't get used

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:13.570
is either to break the ladder somehow,

00:16:13.570 --> 00:16:15.640
which is kind of hard
given the angles involved,

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.680
or to take a long stick and
just try and push the ladder

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:21.450
to the point where it
goes past the vertical

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:22.830
and falls over backwards.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:24.997
So that's very believable.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:33.620
So you do have certain
accounts of machines like these

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:38.960
that will, in some way,
hold something not friendly

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:43.820
over the walls and then
drop it on the attackers.

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:46.560
Usually, they're deployed
against a siege tower

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:48.210
or something a little bit more impressive,

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:50.069
because if it's just
guys coming up a ladder,

00:16:50.069 --> 00:16:52.000
you just drop some stones on them.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:53.940
You often find them in kind of,

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:57.030
or being associated with sailors.

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:58.800
You effectively have a mast

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.650
and then a yard of some description,

00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:03.700
and it will then be
swung out over the wall.

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:06.270
And using rigging that sailors
would be accustomed to,

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:09.040
they can then drop
something on the attackers.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:10.600
The descriptions of these vary.

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:13.300
Sometimes it's kind of
a ring that's on fire.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:16.100
Other times, it's something really heavy.

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:19.440
Other times, you have
just a log with spikes

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:22.130
kind of being hoisted
or tossed over the wall.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:25.100
I'm not an expert of East Asian warfare,

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:28.700
but there's some good elements to this.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:32.170
There's also the obvious
fantasy aspect to it.

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:34.120
So I don't know, maybe 4 out of 10.

